502
00:34:43,780 --> 00:34:47,780
So if I could start with some special education pieces here for you.

503
00:34:47,780 --> 00:34:50,780
Six real things I want you folks to understand.

504
00:34:50,780 --> 00:35:01,780
The key context there is that since its inception, federal IDEA funding has never met its full funding benchmark, which has been 40% since 1981.

505
00:35:01,780 --> 00:35:03,780
I'm going to get into that a little bit more.

506
00:35:03,780 --> 00:35:11,780
Takeaway one in terms of students served under IDEA, there are national trends and New Hampshire trends where those numbers are increasing.

507
00:35:11,780 --> 00:35:15,780
Here in Timber Lane, they have decreased over the past four years.

508
00:35:15,780 --> 00:35:22,780
Two, New Hampshire and Timber Lane are above the national average in terms of total students served.

509
00:35:22,780 --> 00:35:35,780
So think about how those two numbers correlate together is that despite the declining enrollment here in Timber Lane, we still have a higher percentage nationally in terms of what we have for our students served under IDEA.

510
00:35:35,780 --> 00:35:48,780
Three, when we examined all the revenue, the non local revenue, so the non taxpayer revenue that has come in specific to special education, I'll demonstrate for you that it certainly has lost its purchasing power over those four years.

511
00:35:48,780 --> 00:35:57,780
Four, what we learned through this analysis is that the student count, the number of students, is a fairly poor predictor of total special education costs.

512
00:35:57,780 --> 00:36:05,780
And much rather it's dependent upon the individual type, the intensity, and the model to deliver those services.

513
00:36:05,780 --> 00:36:14,780
And lastly, I've alluded to it before, but then 100 state level special education requirements here in New Hampshire that go above and beyond IDEA.

514
00:36:14,780 --> 00:36:22,780
To go into a little bit more detail with these pieces, talked about the 40% benchmark for IDEA funding.

515
00:36:22,780 --> 00:36:29,780
Here you will see a line chart going back to 1977 when IDEA was enacted at the federal level.

516
00:36:29,780 --> 00:36:34,780
It had a benchmark of funding IDEA students by 5%.

517
00:36:34,780 --> 00:36:37,780
It met its benchmark that year.

518
00:36:37,780 --> 00:36:43,780
It funded 5.1% of the average per pupil expenditure.

519
00:36:43,780 --> 00:36:51,780
In 1978, the benchmark was 10%, and the federal government met its benchmark of 10.2% that year.

520
00:36:51,780 --> 00:36:58,780
And every year since then, up until where we are now, the benchmark increased to 40% in 1981.

521
00:36:58,780 --> 00:37:05,780
And you can see the percentage of meeting that benchmark where the federal government has fallen over the course of its tenure.

522
00:37:05,780 --> 00:37:08,780
So two of 49 occasions, it's met its benchmark.

523
00:37:08,780 --> 00:37:10,780
47 of 49, it has not.

524
00:37:10,780 --> 00:37:18,780
If you look closely, the share of the federal government through IDEA is giving school districts equals the exact share it gave in 1978.

525
00:37:18,780 --> 00:37:24,780
And we are on a 20-year decline in the federal share of the average per pupil expenditure.

526
00:37:28,780 --> 00:37:31,780
How does that kind of hit home in terms of New Hampshire here?

527
00:37:31,780 --> 00:37:33,780
Here's one very specific year in New Hampshire.

528
00:37:33,780 --> 00:37:35,780
This is fiscal year 2020.

529
00:37:35,780 --> 00:37:38,780
This is from the New Hampshire general court as a source.

530
00:37:38,780 --> 00:37:50,780
In that year, New Hampshire, if the federal government would meet its 40% benchmark, would have collected $143.8 million in non-local revenue.

531
00:37:50,780 --> 00:37:51,780
It did not.

532
00:37:51,780 --> 00:37:58,780
It collected $51.6 million, leaving a gap of $92.2 million.

533
00:37:58,780 --> 00:38:09,780
And the folks that I know sit on this committee that understand how school funding works in New Hampshire recognizes that then that then falls to the local level for these required services.

534
00:38:09,780 --> 00:38:14,780
Percentage-wise, you can see the statutory benchmark that was provided to New Hampshire in that year.

535
00:38:14,780 --> 00:38:20,780
And you can see the percent of that benchmark that was not provided to New Hampshire.

536
00:38:20,780 --> 00:38:23,780
Here in Timberlane, this is statistics specifically local.

537
00:38:23,780 --> 00:38:25,780
This is looking at student count.

538
00:38:25,780 --> 00:38:31,780
You can see clearly on the right-hand side that the change in students with IDPs is on a decline.

539
00:38:31,780 --> 00:38:36,780
And that decline is outpacing the general decline of the student population, total student population.

540
00:38:36,780 --> 00:38:45,780
We've had a net loss of 65 students served under IDEA in those four years while experiencing a net loss of 125 students.

541
00:38:45,780 --> 00:38:58,780
And again, hitting the revenue, these are the four primary sources of non-local revenue received by school districts for special education.

542
00:38:58,780 --> 00:39:08,780
Special education aid, the first of those is the idea of reimbursement for students who are costing three and a half times the average cost per pupil of state.

543
00:39:08,780 --> 00:39:13,780
IDEA is that federal measure that we had talked about earlier.

544
00:39:13,780 --> 00:39:18,780
Special education aid is a per pupil grant herein as a state aid in New Hampshire.

545
00:39:18,780 --> 00:39:22,780
And Medicaid is also a reimbursement distribution formula.

546
00:39:22,780 --> 00:39:33,780
You can see what's happening with the purchasing power of three or four of those sources of revenue since fiscal 22.

547
00:39:33,780 --> 00:39:41,780
Any pause, Sheila, for questions or things that people want to dig into specific to anything regarding special education trends and takeaways?

548
00:39:41,780 --> 00:39:42,780
Anybody?

549
00:39:42,780 --> 00:39:46,780
Before I went into elementary enrollment.

550
00:39:46,780 --> 00:40:00,780
So I'm hearing you, seeing what you have here related to the decline of special education students and the fact that that is not,

551
00:40:00,780 --> 00:40:04,780
that is not the correct way to measure the needs, right?

552
00:40:04,780 --> 00:40:06,780
You're talking about takeaway four here.

553
00:40:06,780 --> 00:40:07,780
Correct.

554
00:40:07,780 --> 00:40:08,780
Right.

555
00:40:08,780 --> 00:40:12,780
And I know I think you and I have had some conversations about this in the past.

556
00:40:12,780 --> 00:40:19,780
It seems like the correct measurement is more of the height and frequency of interactions.

557
00:40:19,780 --> 00:40:26,780
Is that something that you have data to go over with this committee?

558
00:40:26,780 --> 00:40:28,780
Or is that something that we could see?

559
00:40:28,780 --> 00:40:32,780
We can put it together if we get pretty specific about what we're looking for.

560
00:40:32,780 --> 00:40:40,780
What this generally communicates is that, hypothetically, you have 100 identified students.

561
00:40:40,780 --> 00:40:49,780
And if their need or their service is 15 minutes from a speech pathologist once a week, right?

562
00:40:49,780 --> 00:41:02,780
That child or one of those 100 children has a very different cost for that service for a child who requires five hours of speech therapy a week.

563
00:41:02,780 --> 00:41:08,780
And occupational therapy services and PT services and nursing services.

564
00:41:08,780 --> 00:41:11,780
So those things begin to kind of add up.

565
00:41:11,780 --> 00:41:17,780
So the point here is it's the number of students is just a poor predictor of overall cost.

566
00:41:17,780 --> 00:41:28,780
But yes, there are in terms of the intensity and type of service, I think we could talk and we could be able to generate some metrics about how do we want to look at those pieces of data that we have?

567
00:41:28,780 --> 00:41:31,780
How are we going to classify the intensity of things?

568
00:41:31,780 --> 00:41:34,780
And are we going to look across disabilities?

569
00:41:34,780 --> 00:41:36,780
Are we going to focus in on a specific disability?

570
00:41:36,780 --> 00:41:39,780
There are a lot of different ways to look at that information.

571
00:41:39,780 --> 00:41:40,780
Yeah.

572
00:41:40,780 --> 00:41:56,780
So, I mean, I think the reason I ask is just that, you know, since I started paying attention here, right, over the last two or three years, the topic has come up that, you know, special education costs have gone up significantly, particularly in the last two years.

573
00:41:56,780 --> 00:42:10,780
And so, if we're saying, okay, the number of students is not the correct, you know, justification for that, as we've seen, then we see what that, what it really is, right?

574
00:42:10,780 --> 00:42:12,780
Where is that coming from?

575
00:42:12,780 --> 00:42:13,780
Yeah.

576
00:42:13,780 --> 00:42:23,780
So in terms of tabulating service, which is what I think you're saying, right, that would be the types, the intensity, which is the frequency, and then the model is how you're delivering.

577
00:42:23,780 --> 00:42:26,780
We can describe those kind of models that we have.

578
00:42:26,780 --> 00:42:27,780
Our district is a model, right?

579
00:42:27,780 --> 00:42:29,780
We've got in-district programs, that's a model.

580
00:42:29,780 --> 00:42:32,780
We've got resource rooms, that's a model.

